on\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2\"><polyline points=\"15 3 21 3 21 9\"></polyline><polyline points=\"9 21 3 21 3 15\"></polyline><line x1=\"21\" x2=\"14\" y1=\"3\" y2=\"10\"></line><line x1=\"3\" x2=\"10\" y1=\"21\" y2=\"14\"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>And even this, <strong>Photo 3:</strong></p><div class=\"captioned-image-container\"><figure><a class=\"image-link image2 is-viewable-img\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mfYB!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc68b66a2-dd7c-49ce-82ee-e44f19832461_4032x3024.jpeg\" data-component-name=\"Image2ToDOM\"><div class=\"image2-inset\"><picture><source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mfYB!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc68b66a2-dd7c-49ce-82ee-e44f19832461_4032x3024.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mfYB!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc68b66a2-dd7c-49ce-82ee-e44f19832461_4032x3024.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mfYB!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc68b66a2-dd7c-49ce-82ee-e44f19832461_4032x3024.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mfYB!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc68b66a2-dd7c-49ce-82ee-e44f19832461_4032x3024.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mfYB!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc68b66a2-dd7c-49ce-82ee-e44f19832461_4032x3024.jpeg\" width=\"1456\" height=\"1092\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/c68b66a2-dd7c-49ce-82ee-e44f19832461_4032x3024.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1092,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:2598077,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:false,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}\" class=\"sizing-normal\" alt=\"\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mfYB!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc68b66a2-dd7c-49ce-82ee-e44f19832461_4032x3024.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mfYB!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc68b66a2-dd7c-49ce-82ee-e44f19832461_4032x3024.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mfYB!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc68b66a2-dd7c-49ce-82ee-e44f19832461_4032x3024.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!mfYB!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2Fc68b66a2-dd7c-49ce-82ee-e44f19832461_4032x3024.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\"></picture><div class=\"image-link-expand\"><div class=\"pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset\"><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 20 20\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke=\"var(--color-fg-primary)\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g><path d=\"M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882\"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2\"><polyline points=\"15 3 21 3 21 9\"></polyline><polyline points=\"9 21 3 21 3 15\"></polyline><line x1=\"21\" x2=\"14\" y1=\"3\" y2=\"10\"></line><line x1=\"3\" x2=\"10\" y1=\"21\" y2=\"14\"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>However, of the three photos…I will ask you a question:</p><div class=\"poll-embed\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;id&quot;:199665}\" data-component-name=\"PollToDOM\"></div><p>And, of course…</p><div class=\"poll-embed\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;id&quot;:199666}\" data-component-name=\"PollToDOM\"></div><p>Pictures of brains, I suspect, will be more convincing.   The more colorful, the more convincing. There is some data about this…as far back as 2008:</p><blockquote><p>A series of highly-cited experiments published in 2008 demonstrated a biasing effect of neuroimages on lay perceptions of scientific research.<a class=\"footnote-anchor\" data-component-name=\"FootnoteAnchorToDOM\" id=\"footnote-anchor-1\" href=\"#footnote-1\" target=\"_self\">1</a></p></blockquote><p>Like most splashy science findings, this suffers from a lack of nuance, as subsequent science illuminates:</p><blockquote><p>Experiments 1 through 4 present conceptual and direct replications of some of the original 2008 experiments, and find no evidence of a neuroimage bias. A fifth experiment is reported that confirms that, when laypeople are allowed multiple points of reference (e.g., when directly comparing neuroimagery to other graphical depictions of neurological data), a neuroimage bias can be observed.<a class=\"footnote-anchor\" data-component-name=\"FootnoteAnchorToDOM\" id=\"footnote-anchor-2\" href=\"#footnote-2\" target=\"_self\">2</a></p></blockquote><p>So sometimes, pictures of brains are biasing.  More often, data presented by scientists is hard to understand!</p><p>Not all brain research is as clinically relevant as one might hope:  My favorite such paper was reported with the incomplete title of the original paper: “Neural correlates of interspecies perspective taking.”  Spicy…however, the complete title is a bit more…brat.</p><blockquote><p>The study used a dead salmon as a participant. The actual title is “Neural correlates of interspecies perspective taking in the post-mortem Atlantic Salmon”(Bennett et al., 2009). Whilst the incomplete title is reasonably familiar to common brain research titles, the complete title is quite absurd and questions what these brain research titles actually mean and represent. Although it might sound ridiculous, Bennett et al. do in fact report a functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) study with an Atlantic Salmon as a participant. The dead salmon was informed of the task and was shown several images of humans depicting a certain emotion. Activity in the dead salmon’s brain was recorded using fMRI during the condition in which photographs were shown compared to a rest condition.<a class=\"footnote-anchor\" data-component-name=\"FootnoteAnchorToDOM\" id=\"footnote-anchor-3\" href=\"#footnote-3\" target=\"_self\">3</a></p></blockquote><p>All of which is to say, as I routinely argue, methods matters in interpretation of what to do with any given piece of research.  Brain science is complex, and, often, so is the data presenting. In fact…there are studies on misleading study data presentation…</p><blockquote><p><strong>Methods:</strong></p><p>Participants (n = 329) were randomly assigned to view one of four treatments for four different graph types (bar, line, pie, and bubble) and then asked to answer a question about each graph. Participants were asked to rank the ease with which they read each graph and comment on what they used to respond to the question about each graph.</p><p><strong>Results/Discussion:</strong></p><p>Results show that deceptive tactics caused participants to misinterpret information in the deceptive versus control visualizations across all graph types. Neither graph titles nor previous coursework impacted responses for any of the graphs. Qualitative responses illuminate people's perceptions of graph readability and what information they use to read different types of graphs.</p></blockquote><p>And, blessedly, the science of analyzing sh$itty science is progressing:</p><blockquote><p>Specifically, we analyzed 2227 flawed data visualizations collected from an online gallery and derived a design task-associated taxonomy containing 76 specific design flaws.</p></blockquote><p>And the reasons data is poorly presented is being more accurately characterized:</p><blockquote><p>most existing work characterizes design flaws from the perspective of researchers rather than the perspective of general users. [and], little work has been done to infer why these design flaws occur.<a class=\"footnote-anchor\" data-component-name=\"FootnoteAnchorToDOM\" id=\"footnote-anchor-4\" href=\"#footnote-4\" target=\"_self\">4</a></p></blockquote><p>There is a lot of remarkable science out there.  But to most people, even experts, it can be hard to tell real from “fake.”</p><div class=\"captioned-image-container\"><figure><a class=\"image-link image2 is-viewable-img\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!L4cV!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1d6ab051-fa6f-4971-8828-901160b8324e_4032x3024.jpeg\" data-component-name=\"Image2ToDOM\"><div class=\"image2-inset\"><picture><source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!L4cV!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1d6ab051-fa6f-4971-8828-901160b8324e_4032x3024.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!L4cV!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1d6ab051-fa6f-4971-8828-901160b8324e_4032x3024.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!L4cV!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1d6ab051-fa6f-4971-8828-901160b8324e_4032x3024.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!L4cV!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1d6ab051-fa6f-4971-8828-901160b8324e_4032x3024.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!L4cV!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1d6ab051-fa6f-4971-8828-901160b8324e_4032x3024.jpeg\" width=\"1456\" height=\"1092\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/1d6ab051-fa6f-4971-8828-901160b8324e_4032x3024.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1092,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:2570755,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}\" class=\"sizing-normal\" alt=\"\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!L4cV!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1d6ab051-fa6f-4971-8828-901160b8324e_4032x3024.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!L4cV!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1d6ab051-fa6f-4971-8828-901160b8324e_4032x3024.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!L4cV!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1d6ab051-fa6f-4971-8828-901160b8324e_4032x3024.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!L4cV!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F1d6ab051-fa6f-4971-8828-901160b8324e_4032x3024.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\" loading=\"lazy\"></picture><div class=\"image-link-expand\"><div class=\"pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset\"><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 20 20\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke=\"var(--color-fg-primary)\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g><path d=\"M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882\"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2\"><polyline points=\"15 3 21 3 21 9\"></polyline><polyline points=\"9 21 3 21 3 15\"></polyline><line x1=\"21\" x2=\"14\" y1=\"3\" y2=\"10\"></line><line x1=\"3\" x2=\"10\" y1=\"21\" y2=\"14\"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p>Let’s not forget that compelling story telling is important…and the data visualization might be the one ring for understanding.  But when in doubt, pictures of brains, and compelling story telling together for the win.</p><div class=\"captioned-image-container\"><figure><a class=\"image-link image2 is-viewable-img\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!54s1!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F38cd8a03-e0eb-4d22-807c-5950cb006663_5712x4284.jpeg\" data-component-name=\"Image2ToDOM\"><div class=\"image2-inset\"><picture><source type=\"image/webp\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!54s1!,w_424,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F38cd8a03-e0eb-4d22-807c-5950cb006663_5712x4284.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!54s1!,w_848,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F38cd8a03-e0eb-4d22-807c-5950cb006663_5712x4284.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!54s1!,w_1272,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F38cd8a03-e0eb-4d22-807c-5950cb006663_5712x4284.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!54s1!,w_1456,c_limit,f_webp,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F38cd8a03-e0eb-4d22-807c-5950cb006663_5712x4284.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!54s1!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F38cd8a03-e0eb-4d22-807c-5950cb006663_5712x4284.jpeg\" width=\"1456\" height=\"1941\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/38cd8a03-e0eb-4d22-807c-5950cb006663_5712x4284.jpeg&quot;,&quot;srcNoWatermark&quot;:null,&quot;fullscreen&quot;:null,&quot;imageSize&quot;:null,&quot;height&quot;:1941,&quot;width&quot;:1456,&quot;resizeWidth&quot;:null,&quot;bytes&quot;:3864714,&quot;alt&quot;:null,&quot;title&quot;:null,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;image/jpeg&quot;,&quot;href&quot;:null,&quot;belowTheFold&quot;:true,&quot;topImage&quot;:false,&quot;internalRedirect&quot;:null,&quot;isProcessing&quot;:false,&quot;align&quot;:null,&quot;offset&quot;:false}\" class=\"sizing-normal\" alt=\"\" srcset=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!54s1!,w_424,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F38cd8a03-e0eb-4d22-807c-5950cb006663_5712x4284.jpeg 424w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!54s1!,w_848,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F38cd8a03-e0eb-4d22-807c-5950cb006663_5712x4284.jpeg 848w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!54s1!,w_1272,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F38cd8a03-e0eb-4d22-807c-5950cb006663_5712x4284.jpeg 1272w, https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/$s_!54s1!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F38cd8a03-e0eb-4d22-807c-5950cb006663_5712x4284.jpeg 1456w\" sizes=\"100vw\" loading=\"lazy\"></picture><div class=\"image-link-expand\"><div class=\"pencraft pc-display-flex pc-gap-8 pc-reset\"><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container restack-image\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 20 20\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke=\"var(--color-fg-primary)\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><g><path d=\"M2.53001 7.81595C3.49179 4.73911 6.43281 2.5 9.91173 2.5C13.1684 2.5 15.9537 4.46214 17.0852 7.23684L17.6179 8.67647M17.6179 8.67647L18.5002 4.26471M17.6179 8.67647L13.6473 6.91176M17.4995 12.1841C16.5378 15.2609 13.5967 17.5 10.1178 17.5C6.86118 17.5 4.07589 15.5379 2.94432 12.7632L2.41165 11.3235M2.41165 11.3235L1.5293 15.7353M2.41165 11.3235L6.38224 13.0882\"></path></g></svg></button><button tabindex=\"0\" type=\"button\" class=\"pencraft pc-reset pencraft icon-container view-image\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-maximize2 lucide-maximize-2\"><polyline points=\"15 3 21 3 21 9\"></polyline><polyline points=\"9 21 3 21 3 15\"></polyline><line x1=\"21\" x2=\"14\" y1=\"3\" y2=\"10\"></line><line x1=\"3\" x2=\"10\" y1=\"21\" y2=\"14\"></line></svg></button></div></div></div></a></figure></div><p class=\"button-wrapper\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/subscribe?&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Subscribe now&quot;,&quot;action&quot;:null,&quot;class&quot;:null}\" data-component-name=\"ButtonCreateButton\"><a class=\"button primary\" href=\"https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/subscribe?\"><span>Subscribe now</span></a></p><p></p><div class=\"footnote\" data-component-name=\"FootnoteToDOM\"><a id=\"footnote-1\" href=\"#footnote-anchor-1\" class=\"footnote-number\" contenteditable=\"false\" target=\"_self\">1</a><div class=\"footnote-content\"><p>Dumit, J. (1999). Objective brains, prejudicial images. <em>Science in Context</em>, <em>12</em>(1), 173-201.</p><p></p></div></div><div class=\"footnote\" data-component-name=\"FootnoteToDOM\"><a id=\"footnote-2\" href=\"#footnote-anchor-2\" class=\"footnote-number\" contenteditable=\"false\" target=\"_self\">2</a><div class=\"footnote-content\"><p>Schweitzer, N. J., Baker, D. A., &amp; Risko, E. F. (2013). Fooled by the brain: Re-examining the influence of neuroimages. <em>Cognition</em>, <em>129</em>(3), 501-511.</p><p></p></div></div><div class=\"footnote\" data-component-name=\"FootnoteToDOM\"><a id=\"footnote-3\" href=\"#footnote-anchor-3\" class=\"footnote-number\" contenteditable=\"false\" target=\"_self\">3</a><div class=\"footnote-content\"><p>Nelissen, A. F. (2021). <em>How we became our brains: An analysis of the relation between brain imaging technologies and beliefs in brain research</em> (Master's thesis).</p><p></p></div></div><div class=\"footnote\" data-component-name=\"FootnoteToDOM\"><a id=\"footnote-4\" href=\"#footnote-anchor-4\" class=\"footnote-number\" contenteditable=\"false\" target=\"_self\">4</a><div class=\"footnote-content\"><p>Lan, X., &amp; Liu, Y. (2024). \" I Came Across a Junk\": Understanding Design Flaws of Data Visualization from the Public's Perspective. <em>arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.11497</em>.</p><p></p></div></div>","text":"The Frontier Psychiatrists is a daily-enough health themed newsletter. I’ve been at the Neuromodec conference in NYC this week. It is a neuromodulation conference that leans in heavily to research on the brain. There are a lot of slides, and a lot of posters.\n\nScientists do us all a great service when they detangle the maze of the brain to provide us with accessible answers that can relieve suffering. However, there is a theme I noticed.\n\nThis is science:\n\nPhoto 1:\n\nIt is about a modality that might improve memory (HD-tACS). Now, compare that science to this… Photo 2:\n\nAnd even this, Photo 3:\n\nHowever, of the three photos…I will ask you a question:\n\nAnd, of course…\n\nPictures of brains, I suspect, will be more convincing. The more colorful, the more convincing. There is some data about this…as far back as 2008:\n\nA series of highly-cited experiments published in 2008 demonstrated a biasing effect of neuroimages on lay perceptions of scientific research. 1\n\nLike most splashy science findings, this suffers from a lack of nuance, as subsequent science illuminates:\n\nExperiments 1 through 4 present conceptual and direct replications of some of the original 2008 experiments, and find no evidence of a neuroimage bias. A fifth experiment is reported that confirms that, when laypeople are allowed multiple points of reference (e.g., when directly comparing neuroimagery to other graphical depictions of neurological data), a neuroimage bias can be observed. 2\n\nSo sometimes, pictures of brains are biasing. More often, data presented by scientists is hard to understand!\n\nNot all brain research is as clinically relevant as one might hope: My favorite such paper was reported with the incomplete title of the original paper: “Neural correlates of interspecies perspective taking.” Spicy…however, the complete title is a bit more…brat.\n\nThe study used a dead salmon as a participant. The actual title is “Neural correlates of interspecies perspective taking in the post-mortem Atlantic Salmon”(Bennett et al., 2009). Whilst the incomplete title is reasonably familiar to common brain research titles, the complete title is quite absurd and questions what these brain research titles actually mean and represent. Although it might sound ridiculous, Bennett et al. do in fact report a functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) study with an Atlantic Salmon as a participant. The dead salmon was informed of the task and was shown several images of humans depicting a certain emotion. Activity in the dead salmon’s brain was recorded using fMRI during the condition in which photographs were shown compared to a rest condition. 3\n\nAll of which is to say, as I routinely argue, methods matters in interpretation of what to do with any given piece of research. Brain science is complex, and, often, so is the data presenting. In fact…there are studies on misleading study data presentation…\n\nMethods:\n\nParticipants (n = 329) were randomly assigned to view one of four treatments for four different graph types (bar, line, pie, and bubble) and then asked to answer a question about each graph. Participants were asked to rank the ease with which they read each graph and comment on what they used to respond to the question about each graph.\n\nResults/Discussion:\n\nResults show that deceptive tactics caused participants to misinterpret information in the deceptive versus control visualizations across all graph types. Neither graph titles nor previous coursework impacted responses for any of the graphs. Qualitative responses illuminate people's perceptions of graph readability and what information they use to read different types of graphs.\n\nAnd, blessedly, the science of analyzing sh$itty science is progressing:\n\nSpecifically, we analyzed 2227 flawed data visualizations collected from an online gallery and derived a design task-associated taxonomy containing 76 specific design flaws.\n\nAnd the reasons data is poorly presented is being more accurately characterized:\n\nmost existing work characterizes design flaws from the perspective of researchers rather than the perspective of general users. [and], little work has been done to infer why these design flaws occur. 4\n\nThere is a lot of remarkable science out there. But to most people, even experts, it can be hard to tell real from “fake.”\n\nLet’s not forget that compelling story telling is important…and the data visualization might be the one ring for understanding. But when in doubt, pictures of brains, and compelling story telling together for the win.\n\nSubscribe now\n\n1 Dumit, J. (1999). Objective brains, prejudicial images. Science in Context , 12 (1), 173-201.\n\n2 Schweitzer, N. J., Baker, D. A., & Risko, E. F. (2013). Fooled by the brain: Re-examining the influence of neuroimages. Cognition , 129 (3), 501-511.\n\n3 Nelissen, A. F. (2021). How we became our brains: An analysis of the relation between brain imaging technologies and beliefs in brain research (Master's thesis).\n\n4 Lan, X., & Liu, Y. (2024). \" I Came Across a Junk\": Understanding Design Flaws of Data Visualization from the Public's Perspective. arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.11497 ."}